A||N||10||Freshj||Help! Low Oil Pressure||||1||
Z||000000||Freshj||11-09-2001||10:35 PM||||So heres the Spiel. I just bought a 86 Se w/ the 2.8. It had been parked for 3 years now. I drove it one block to the Jiffy Lube. Got an oil change (5w30). When she warms up 200-220F it has low oil pressure (light flickers) lower then 1000rpms. Now most peoples resonse to this is that the Bearings are shot. Well the last time it was driven 3 years ago it had no problms what so ever. This leads me to believe it isn't that. There's some serious gunk in the heads. A couple people told me to flush it w/ Kerosene/Fuel oil. A couple of other people told me that that would cause some serious knocking. Others say Put 10w30 and a high volume fule pump in. Could that gunk cause low pressure? Any help would be greatly appreciated as I am afraid to drive it w/ it in it's current state. ||||reg||1
Z||000001||reddog||11-09-2001||11:01 PM||||I have the same problem exactly when I bought my car the pcv tube wasn't even connected in the back. Looked inside the oil filler cap and it looks pretty nasty also. Also have same problem with the light so I am very interested to here everyone's thoughts on this.I don't know but seems to me if the oil pressure goes down after the engine warms up that the problem is in the oil pressure sending unit. But I don't know all that much about it.If you find out what the cause is please let me know also.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by reddog (edited 11-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Freshj||11-09-2001||11:17 PM||||Oh, I forgot to mention the Engine purrs. It makes great power and has no sort of noises. And the oil gague never really stays put its always vibrating a little. Not a bouncing but more of a shake. And someone mentioned A Oil pressure relief valve being stuck?<p>[This message has been edited by Freshj (edited 11-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||PaulJK||11-10-2001||01:15 AM||||When I bought my 1985 SE with V-6 and 4 speed the oil pressure gauge would go off scale high over 1500 rpm. Sometimes it would violently slap both ends offscale low & offscale high - you could hear the needle hit! The engine seemed to run too well to have a serious problem. Everyone i talked to said factory gauges are junk, so I changed the gauge & sending unit. Reads steady 60 at idle now  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. BE WARNED - the factory oil pressure sender will allow the car to start if the fuel pump relay dies and also shut down the fuel pump if oil press. goes to zero. You will loose both these features if you use an aftermarket sender.||||reg||1
Z||000004||maryjane||11-10-2001||02:17 AM||||The reason the gunk on the heads can cause low oil press is: That stuff sat on the cyl hd for 3 yrs. It probably dried out, maybe even flaked some. When you started driving it again, it got washed down into the pan and partially plugged the oil screen. But first, check the oil press with a good gauge, it may be you are getting a false reading. There are several thinmgs you can put in the pan to dissolve the stuff, and then drain it out. One even says on the can to drain the oil and pour the stuff in, then run it for a few minutes at idle, then drain it and refill with oil after changing the filter. Use your own judgement. I still have serious questions about the oil press sw shutting down the eng. Maybe if the fuel pump relay is bad. The relay on my 84 4cyl is good, I disconnected the stock oil press switch, the engine kept running. Dunno. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||11-10-2001||04:54 AM||||you will almost always lose some oil pressure after it warms up, it gets thinner. You could also try running 10/40 oil. I use 5/50 castrol synthetic in mine. Id suspect that either your gauge or sending unit is defective though. There are some contact points in the gauge that will corode and give false, intermittent readings too. clean them up with some sandpaper, spray contact cleaner.||||reg||1
Z||000006||reddog||11-10-2001||06:30 PM||||Hey rogergarrison, can you give us more details on how to clean the contact points you were referring to ( Where are they, What do they look like?)<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Picturenew_sig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Mach10||11-10-2001||06:45 PM||||I'm in the "gunked up motor" opinion club. Toss a can of engine oil system flush, and run for 10 minutes. Drain it, (note the ebony black color of oil) and change oil/filter. If that doesn't help, I'd suggest pulling the pan off and manually de-gunking the oil pump. Replace it if you'd like  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000008||Fierowrecker||11-10-2001||08:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Freshj:<BR><B>So heres the Spiel. I just bought a 86 Se w/ the 2.8. It had been parked for 3 years now. I drove it one block to the Jiffy Lube. Got an oil change (5w30). When she warms up 200-220F it has low oil pressure (light flickers) lower then 1000rpms. Now most peoples resonse to this is that the Bearings are shot. Well the last time it was driven 3 years ago it had no problms what so ever. This leads me to believe it isn't that. There's some serious gunk in the heads. A couple people told me to flush it w/ Kerosene/Fuel oil. A couple of other people told me that that would cause some serious knocking. Others say Put 10w30 and a high volume fule pump in. Could that gunk cause low pressure? Any help would be greatly appreciated as I am afraid to drive it w/ it in it's current state. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey Freshj!<BR>It's me, crash...<BR>Didn't know you were running 5W30, switch to 10W40 AFTER you flush the engine...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000009||rogergarrison||11-10-2001||09:18 PM||||if you pull out the gauge, there are some copper tangs at the edge that make the ground (i think) just see if there dirty /corroded. make them shiny, and maybe bend them a little and reinstall.||||reg||1
Z||000010||stsmithgt||11-11-2001||06:33 AM||||I changed my oil sending unit a couple weeks ago.  It had been sitting on my dresser for about a year. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">  <BR>Anyway, it stopped the needle from dancing around at idle.  It's still a little jerky so I think my gauge needs some help too.  I took so long to replace it because I have an ear out for that clatter at all, uh... most of the time. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  Plus I didn't realize how easy it was to change. DOH!<BR>I've been tempted to add an aftermarket mechanical gauge.  I'd probably leave that one in the engine compartment somewhere to keep the stock looking dash.  It wouldn't hurt to have a second <I>accurate</I>gauge. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif"><P>------------------<BR>My work of art 4..s..money. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/4money.jpg"> ||||reg||1
